%T A397434 155363,155363,354331,721259,1597235,1597235,3439627,14969435,
%U A397434 33096195,33096195,70538355,145422675,300540195,300540195,601080391,4294967295,9756737667,9756737667
%N A397434 Number of monomials in the algebraic normal form (Zhegalkin polynomial over GF(2)) of the Boolean function that is 1 if and only if at least ceiling(n/2) of its n variables are 1.
%C A397434 Proof of the formula a(2^k+1) = 2^(2^k) - 1: by Lucas's theorem the ANF coefficient at degree d is the parity of the number of submasks j of d with j <= 2^(k-1); that number is 2^w + [bit k-1 of d is set], where w counts the bits of d in positions 0..k-2, so the coefficient is 1 exactly for d in {0, 2^k} together with 2^(k-1) < d < 2^k. Summing binomial(2^k+1, d) over that set gives 2^(2^k) by the symmetry of the binomial row, and removing the term d = 0 gives the formula.
%H A397434 Tanguy Gautier Loïc Le Mer, <a href="/A397434/b397434.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..1000</a>
%H A397434 Pierrick Méaux, <a href="https://eprint.iacr.org/2019/999">On the Fast Algebraic Immunity of Majority Functions</a>, Cryptology ePrint Archive, Report 2019/999 (studies the ANF of majority functions).
%F A397434 a(2^k+1) = 2^(2^k) - 1 for k >= 1.
%F A397434 Conjecture: a(n) = a(n+1) if and only if n == 2 (mod 4). Verified for n <= 998.
%e A397434 For n = 3 the function is the majority of x, y, z, whose algebraic normal form is x*y + x*z + y*z over GF(2); it has 3 monomials, so a(3) = 3.
%o A397434 (Python)
%o A397434 from math import comb
%o A397434 def a(n):
%o A397434     t = (n + 1) // 2
%o A397434     return sum(comb(n, d) for d in range(1, n + 1)
%o A397434                if sum(comb(d, k) for k in range(t)) % 2)
%Y A397434 Cf. A000225.
%K A397434 nonn,new
%O A397434 1,2
